  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 124 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nA gradual warming trend will continue to occur across the area \nthis week as upper level ridging remains in place much of the \nweek. Highs will warm a few degrees each day through the 60s \nwith highs in the lower 70s possible by Friday. \n\nSurface high pressure will remain over or just south of the area\nthrough much of the week. The surface high will also limit \nmoisture return. The ensemble model members show an upper level \ntrough move through the region Wednesday into Thursday. Winds \nmay increase some on Wednesday ahead of the trough, with gusts \nup 25 mph at times otherwise light winds will generally occur \nmuch of this week. There are still some minor difference in the \nmodels on the exact track of the trough Wednesday into Thursday.\nThey all have it lifting to the northeast across the plains off\nto the northeast. The further northern tracks keeps the area \ndry as the further southern tracks brings some low rain chances \n(10-20%) for a few showers to the area late Wednesday into \nThursday. A drier air mass will be in place across the area and \nthe better lift will be to the north so if any rain can occur it\nwill be light and likely scattered in nature, may not be much \nmore than sprinkles if any rain occurs at all. \n\nAn upper level trough is expected to move east out of the\nsouthwestern CONUS late this week and lift to the northeast\nacross the region this weekend. There remains differences on the\nmodels on the track, timing and structure of this system.\nMoisture returning will start to return out of Texas into the\ncentral Plains late this week into this weekend. The 12Z models\nare trending further north with the the track of the low. A cold\nfront will move southeast into and through the area with this\nsystem, but with the better moisture return to the west and\nbetter lift trending north it is possible scattered showers and\na few storms develop along the front to the west/northwest of\nthe area with coverage decreasing to the southeast and therefore\nall locations may not be impacted. The 12Z NBM has lowered PoPs\nwith this system to 30 to 45% with the better chances across \nthe northwestern portions of the area. The pressure gradient \nwill increase across the area southwest of the surface low late \nFriday into Saturday which will result in the potential for some\ngusts up to around 30mph at times, especially along and \nnorthwest of I-44.",
